  <li>Set max_links to a reasonable value (the default is 50; the maximum is 200).</li>
  <li>Use concurrency of 10–20 for broken link checks.</li>
  <li>Run audits during off-peak hours.</li>
  <li>Cache audit results for at least 24 hours unless you need real-time monitoring.</li>
  <li>Set up alerts for critical issues: missing titles, broken links on key pages, schema errors.</li>
  <li>Version your audit scripts.</li>
  <li>Monitor API usage in the Ollagraph dashboard.</li>
  <li>Test new endpoints on a staging URL first.</li>
  <li>Combine SEO audit results with AEO audit endpoints for a complete picture.</li>
</ul>

<h3>When to Run Audits</h3>
<pre><code>Frequency | Use Case
Every deployment | CI/CD pipeline check for critical pages
Daily | Ecommerce product pages, landing pages
Weekly | Full site audit for content-driven sites
Monthly | Enterprise portals with stable content
On-demand | After site migrations, redesigns, or CMS updates</code></pre>

<h3>Optimizing for Speed</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Run audits in parallel, not sequentially. Nine parallel calls take about as long as the slowest one.</li>
  <li>Use use_residential_proxy only when needed. Datacenter IPs are faster.</li>
  <li>For broken link audits, set max_links to the number you actually need. Checking 200 links when you need 25 wastes time.</li>
  <li>Each endpoint fetches the page on its own, so calling all nine against one URL means nine fetches. Run only the checks you need.</li>
</ul>

<h2>12. Common Mistakes</h2>

<ol>
  <li>
    <strong>Running Endpoints Sequentially</strong>
    <p>Nine sequential calls take as long as all nine added together. Nine parallel calls take about as long as the slowest one. Use asyncio (Python), Promise.all (Node.js), or curl_parallel (shell).</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Ignoring the Redirect Chain Endpoint</strong>
    <p>Most SEOs check the final URL and call it done. The redirect chain endpoint reveals intermediate hops that dilute link equity.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Not Setting max_links on Broken Link Audits</strong>
    <p>The default of 50 links is reasonable for most pages. But on a sitemap-style page with thousands of links, only the first 50 (or up to 200, if you raise it) are checked. Set max_links to match your actual needs, and audit very link-heavy pages in parts.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Treating Schema Validation as a Syntax Check</strong>
    <p>Valid JSON-LD can still be ineligible for rich results. The schema-validate endpoint checks each recognized schema.org type for its required properties and lists the rich-result types the page is eligible for. A Recipe block without recipeIngredient, for example, parses as valid JSON but is reported as missing_fields and not eligible.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Auditing Only the Homepage</strong>
    <p>The homepage is usually the most optimized page. Real issues live on product pages, blog posts, and archived content. Audit your full URL set.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Forgetting Mixed Content After HTTPS Migration</strong>
    <p>Old templates and content can keep referencing HTTP images long after an HTTPS migration. Run the mixed-content endpoint across your full URL set, not just the pages you changed.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Not Checking Snippet Candidates</strong>
    <p>Featured snippets drive 42% of click-throughs on informational queries. If your content isn't formatted as a paragraph, list, table, or definition, you can't win position zero.</p>
  </li>
</ol>

